        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "800b93f1-8332-5df7-9b61-11c287002362",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Queens, Queens County, Staten Island, Flushing Meadows–Corona Park, Flushing Bay, LaGuardia Airport\n- Roads: I-678 (Van Wyck Expressway), Grand Central Parkway, Union Turnpike\n- People: Charles W. Leng\n- Roles: Borough historian\n- Topics: Population, area, foreign-born residents\n\nFacts:\n- Since 1899, the borough of Queens has been coterminous with Queens County.\n- Charles William Leng (1859–1941) was an American naturalist and historian associated with Staten Island, New York.\n- From 1923 until the 1930s, Charles W. Leng served as the borough historian of Staten Island.\n- In 2016, Queens had a census-estimated population of 2,333,054, making it the second-most populous borough after Brooklyn.\n- In 2016, approximately 48% of Queens residents were foreign-born.\n- In 2016, Staten Island had a census-estimated population of 476,015, making it the least populated borough.\n- Staten Island is the third-largest borough in area at 58 square miles.\n- Flushing Meadows–Corona Park is located in Queens between I-678 (Van Wyck Expressway) and the Grand Central Parkway.\n- Flushing Meadows–Corona Park stretches from Flushing Bay at the southern edge of LaGuardia Airport to Union Turnpike.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5656ee6e-c95f-50b1-8104-76e277b63109",
        "text": "Page: Queens. Sentence: Coterminous with Queens County since 1899, the borough of Queens is the second-largest in population (after Brooklyn), with a census-estimated 2,333,054 residents in 2016, approximately 48% of them foreign-born.\nPage: Staten Island. Sentence: With a 2016 Census-estimated population of 476,015, Staten Island is the least populated of the boroughs but is the third-largest in area at 58 sqmi .\nPage: Flushing Meadows–Corona Park. Sentence: Located in the borough of Queens, it is between I-678 (Van Wyck Expressway) and the Grand Central Parkway and stretches from Flushing Bay, at the southern edge of LaGuardia Airport, to Union Turnpike.\nPage: Charles W. Leng. Sentence: Charles William Leng (1859 – 1941) was an American naturalist and historian especially associated with Staten Island, New York, where he was the borough historian from 1923 until the 1930s.",
